WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.730
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here, and today
I wanna talk about drills, quizzes, tests.

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:09.660
Essentially, I'm against them
and I'm gonna explain why.

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:12.000
Now, I'm not entirely against them.

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:13.350
I think some enga...

00:00:13.355 --> 00:00:16.620
any engagement with the
language is a good thing.

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:22.560
However, if the drill or the quiz or
the test leaves you scratching your

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:27.420
head, trying to think of the answer,
you know, reaching back into your

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:32.520
memory, trying to remember rules, trying
to remember endings, those are bad.

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:39.000
The only test that you should be doing or
drill with regard to languages should be

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:42.660
those that are easy, can't get them wrong.

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:43.230
All right.

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:50.760
And to me, a prime example is the matching
pairs that a number of sites have.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:55.050
We have them on LingQ and also the sort
of reassembling of a sentence that we

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:57.060
do and that other sites probably do.

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:01.200
The reason they're good is you
can't really get them wrong.

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:07.800
So the matching pairs, let's say we
have three pairs, and you're supposed

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:11.760
to link up this word with that word,
but it's done on the computer screen.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:14.490
You pick a word, it becomes blue.

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:18.660
You look for the corresponding word in
the target language, or if it's in the

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:22.485
target language, you look for your own
language or some other language,  and

00:01:22.485 --> 00:01:27.390
if you connect them and if that is the
correct answer, they both turn green.

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:30.390
If it's not the right
answer, they turn pink.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:32.100
So if it turns pink, what do you do?

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:32.700
You with...

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:33.750
you retreat.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:37.710
You retreat and you try to match
that word with something else.

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:40.800
So there's no great harm
done if you get it wrong.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.510
You don't have to wait for the answer.

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:44.940
Uh, and I find that very gratifying.

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:45.570
I'll get 'em wrong.

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:46.170
It's no big deal.

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:49.050
I'll go back and and
I, and it's made easy.

00:01:49.054 --> 00:01:54.090
So if there are three pairs and I
get two of the pairs, I know that

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:55.979
the other pair, that's who they are.

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:57.509
So easy.

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:01.590
And also not too many:
easy, not too many, quick.

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:07.215
Those are the only kinds of tests that are
worth doing because they, they might help

00:02:07.215 --> 00:02:10.544
you remember the words, but beyond that,
they give you a sense of satisfaction.

00:02:10.725 --> 00:02:11.895
I'm not beaten up.

00:02:12.015 --> 00:02:16.245
I don't take 10 questions and get
six of them right, and four of

00:02:16.245 --> 00:02:18.405
them wrong, which is discouraging.

00:02:18.734 --> 00:02:23.865
In fact, when I have, uh, in the past
had language textbooks with, you know,

00:02:24.105 --> 00:02:28.275
all kinds of exercises and drills
in the chapter, I won't do them.

00:02:28.635 --> 00:02:33.315
But instead, I will go to the back of
the book where the answers and I'll go

00:02:33.315 --> 00:02:38.625
through the answers, because typically in
these language textbooks, they'll try and

00:02:38.625 --> 00:02:41.595
drill you on a specific aspect of grammar.

00:02:41.774 --> 00:02:47.655
So the answers will provide you with
10 examples, concentrated examples

00:02:47.804 --> 00:02:49.995
of that particular pattern in use.

00:02:50.054 --> 00:02:55.334
So I don't mind looking through 10
examples of that pattern, but I won't do

00:02:55.424 --> 00:02:59.885
the drill where they ask you to, you know,
fill in the blank or whatever it might be.

00:03:00.154 --> 00:03:01.200
I don't want any part of that.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:02.370
I find it's discouraging.

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:03.239
It's a waste of time.

00:03:03.269 --> 00:03:04.320
I don't think it's helpful.

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:10.410
Uh, the other thing that I find useful
as a, as a, an exercise, as I say,

00:03:10.415 --> 00:03:12.390
is this reassembling of a sentence.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:16.200
Again, because it's fun to do, if you
get it wrong, you don't get that little

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:18.269
smiley telling you that you did it right.

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:20.760
You have to keep playing with
it until you get it right.

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:28.280
That, to me, is easy and it doesn't have
the same stress or negativity associated

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:34.665
with it as, you know, doing 10, uh,
providing 10 answers to something, filling

00:03:34.665 --> 00:03:40.335
in the blanks, uh, 10 exercises fill in
the blanks or for everyone, you gotta

00:03:40.335 --> 00:03:41.955
flip to the back of the page and stuff.

00:03:42.435 --> 00:03:45.795
This way you play with it until you
get it right and then you get a little

00:03:45.795 --> 00:03:51.825
smiley, sense satisfaction because it's
very important in any endeavor to have

00:03:51.825 --> 00:03:53.745
a sense that you actually succeeded.

00:03:54.345 --> 00:04:01.335
And it's surprising how even small
successes in whatever activity put

00:04:01.335 --> 00:04:03.015
us in a positive frame of mind.

00:04:03.165 --> 00:04:09.405
Not only for that activity, but generally
we have this feeling of wellbeing if

00:04:09.410 --> 00:04:13.875
we accomplished some task and we did
it, and we did it with some difficulty

00:04:13.875 --> 00:04:18.225
and perhaps a little bit of, you know,
the odd minor hiccup, but we did it.

00:04:18.230 --> 00:04:23.056
And to the extent that we can create
that kind of a feeling, we are

00:04:23.495 --> 00:04:26.835
encouraging the learner even though
the bulk of the learning takes

00:04:26.835 --> 00:04:28.215
place from listening and reading.

00:04:28.724 --> 00:04:34.424
The idea that some kinds of activities,
you know, the matching pairs, the

00:04:34.424 --> 00:04:38.025
assembling of the sentences that
they are in themselves enjoyable.

00:04:38.025 --> 00:04:43.304
So it's an enjoyable form of engagement
with the language and it's something

00:04:43.304 --> 00:04:48.794
that I'll spend 10% of my time on
picking up the nuts and bolts, uh,

00:04:49.315 --> 00:04:51.674
recognizing that the bulk of my
learning is still gonna be through the

00:04:51.674 --> 00:04:56.460
listening and reading, but I don't mind
doing those kinds of call them drills.

00:04:56.730 --> 00:04:59.580
As long as they are easy,
there's not too many of them.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:01.980
Uh, I don't have to scratch my brain.

00:05:02.490 --> 00:05:06.570
And typically I'll do them immediately
after studying a piece of content.

00:05:06.570 --> 00:05:10.860
So when I do the matching pairs or the
assembling of the sentence in LingQ,

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:16.980
I do it after having studied that
particular sentence in sentence mode in

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:22.409
LingQ so that the, uh, exercises that
I am doing are relevant to content that

00:05:22.409 --> 00:05:23.915
I'm actually listening to and reading.

00:05:24.395 --> 00:05:30.075
To me, the worst kind of drill or, or
exercise or or quiz is when you are

00:05:30.075 --> 00:05:35.984
quizzed on random lists of vocabulary or
random rules of grammar, not related to

00:05:36.135 --> 00:05:40.875
specific content that you are listening
to or reading so that it's completely

00:05:40.875 --> 00:05:43.755
disconnected from meaningful input.

00:05:44.055 --> 00:05:48.465
So again, my rules are do stuff
that's easy, not too many at the

00:05:48.465 --> 00:05:53.265
time, connected with content you're
interested in, and let that be 10,

00:05:53.265 --> 00:05:55.605
15, 20% of your learning activity.

00:05:55.784 --> 00:06:00.555
But as a general rule where those
conditions that I described don't exist,

00:06:01.065 --> 00:06:06.945
don't do drills, quizzes, and tests,
unless you're told to at school of course.

00:06:07.544 --> 00:06:08.055
That's it.

00:06:08.055 --> 00:06:08.805
Thank you for listening.

00:06:08.805 --> 00:06:09.275
Bye for now.

